 Google actually doesn't have  too much to worry about just yet I would  say mostly because the survey that they  did kind of found that more often than  not people would still use Google in  addition to tick tock however for those  who only rely on Tick Tock as the like  the key source of information that can  be a little Troublesome because not all  of the results that they might get  immediately on the app are always  accurate  okay so what makes it problematic then  for the people who are turning to to  tick tock for information  yeah so tick tock's algorithm gives you  information based on your interests and  what videos are like the most popular at  the time when it comes to those kinds of  videos they don't necessarily always  have to come from reliable sources so if  you're using the app to search for  information such as let's say health  advice but you're not really getting it  from an actual nutritionist then you  might be using some of these skills and  these like tips from someone that might  actually do you more harm than good so  you want to make sure that you're going  off platform to do a little extra  research to make sure you're getting  information from an accurate interested  Source did you find any examples of  where that was happening  so an example of this would be an  avocado hack which was often circulating  around the app and which different users  would see like videos of someone placing  an avocado in a container filled with  water and putting in their fridge and it  was supposedly allowing their avocados  to last a little bit longer without  turning brown however the FDA eventually  had to come out and say that hey that's  actually not really safe due to  pathogens in the water that could  actually cause all kinds of illnesses  you know other social media platforms  have been criticized for the amount of  misinformation that's on their platform  is that something Tick-Tock has to deal  with as well and how was it addressing  that  most 